{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent upward bias in the true series vs model: forecasts are systematically too low across most horizons (roughly +2 to +8 EUR), especially during strong uptrends. Apply an additive uplift that increases with recent momentum/volatility.",
    "Trend/level lag: the model reacts too slowly to regime shifts upward (breakout from ~70 to ~80+). Use a momentum correction: if last 5–10d slope is strongly positive, raise the entire path and steepen the first 1–2 weeks.",
    "Horizon-dependent underestimation: error tends to grow with horizon (weeks 2–4 most underpriced). Apply a term-structure correction where the uplift is larger for days 10–25 than for days 1–5.",
    "False mean-reversion: model repeatedly predicts pullbacks/flattening while realized prices keep grinding higher. Reduce mean-reversion strength when price is making higher highs and higher lows (trend filter).",
    "End-of-horizon overshoot/undershoot pattern: in some cases the model drifts down late (e.g., predicts ~68–71) while realized remains ~78–80; avoid late-horizon bearish drift unless confirmed by recent downside momentum. Impose a floor tied to recent 10–20d average plus a fraction of recent trend.",
    "Volatility underestimation during rallies: realized swings and upside continuation exceed forecast amplitude. Inflate forecast variance/upside tails when recent realized volatility rises and price is above key moving averages."
  ]
}
